FINDINGS:

1. [BLOCKER] src/vfs.rs:382 — `file_key` decides a path “names a directory” only from `path.ends_with('/')`, so a dot-spelled directory on a file is lost by `normalize` and the operation lands on the file. `files.write("note/.", …)` overwrites `note`, `files.read("note/.")` reads it, and `files.delete("note/.")` deletes it; a native `std::fs` call with the same text is `NotADirectory`. The same fall-through affects `append`, `copy` and `rename` because they all share `file_key`. — Why it matters: this is silent data replacement/deletion in the playground for a spelling native rejects, contradicting the phase’s central “both builds agree” invariant, and it is exactly the trailing-separator class the resume claims to close. — Fix: have `file_key` detect that the original path ended in `.`/`..` (or that `normalize` dropped a final component), and treat that as `names_directory` just like a trailing `/`; add a test `note/.` on an existing file for read/write/delete/copy/rename. — VERIFIED: traced `normalize` at src/vfs.rs:349-370 (folds `note/.` to `note`) and `file_key` at src/vfs.rs:380-403 (`names_directory = path.ends_with('/')` at 382, then falls through to `Ok(key)` at 402); native `std::fs::write("note/.")` returns ENOTDIR/`NotADirectory`.

2. [MAJOR] src/vfs.rs:212 and src/vfs.rs:235 — `exists` and `is_file` have the same gap: they only consult `path.ends_with('/')`, so `files.exists("file.txt/.")` returns `yes` and `is_file("file.txt/.")` returns `yes`, while native `Path::exists` and `Path::is_file` return false because `file.txt` is not a directory. — Why it matters: programs branch on these predicates, and the two builds now disagree for a spelling the phase explicitly normalized for. — Fix: distinguish “the original path asked for a directory” (trailing `/` or a final `.`/`..`) in one helper used by `exists`, `is_file`, and `file_key`; add the missing cases to `edge_a_trailing_separator_does_not_name_a_file`. — Checked by reading src/vfs.rs:208-242; no existing test pins the dot-spelled case (the test at tests/wasm_test.rs:1362 uses `data/.` only when `data` is an implied directory).

3. [MINOR] src/vfs.rs:306 — the in-memory `read_dir` treats `/` like any named directory: an empty store (or a store holding only relative keys) makes `path.list("/")` return `NotFound`, while the native `read_dir("/")` succeeds because the root always exists. The working directory was special-cased (`.`, `./`), but the root was not. — Why it matters: `path.list` is the new surface, and this is an uncovered platform divergence in an edge case the report’s matrix claims is handled. — Fix: return the listing (or an empty listing) for key `/` rather than `NotFound`, and add a wasm test for `path.list("/")` on an empty store.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
